./DECISIONS.md:23:**Reasoning:** Claude Code itself is a Node CLI, and its hooks (`PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `Stop`) are plain shell commands — so the integration cost between Grasp and Claude Code is roughly equal regardless of Grasp's own language. Given that, the deciding factors are downstream: `npm install -g` keeps Grasp in the exact install path its target users (Claude Code users) already have configured, with no extra runtime to bootstrap. The Node ecosystem also maps cleanly onto what the brief actually needs — `better-sqlite3` for the local store (3.5), `execa` for shelling out to `claude -p` and parsing its JSON output (3.3), and mature diff-parsing/syntax-highlighting libraries for the TUI (3.4) — without requiring custom tooling to glue them together.
./DECISIONS.md:33:**Reasoning:** JSON needs no extra parsing dependency in a Node/TS project (`JSON.parse` is enough), and it's the format Claude Code's own settings already use, so it stays consistent with the ecosystem Grasp lives in. A two-tier global + per-repo merge matches how the brief actually talks about config (ignore patterns, caps) — some of it is clearly personal/global (cost cap, gate mode) and some is naturally project-specific (ignore patterns for a given repo's generated paths), so a single flat file would force one scope to win awkwardly over the other.
./DECISIONS.md:43:**Reasoning:** The brief's §5.2 explicitly calls for a size threshold but gives no numbers, and also explicitly delegates "worth asking about" judgment to the model in §3.3 rather than a static list. Splitting the work this way keeps Phase 4 doing only what's mechanical and nearly free (line counting), while leaving the judgment call — which the brief is emphatic can't generalize as a static list — entirely to the one place designed to make it. The lower bound (3 lines) exists purely to avoid burning a generation call on a trivial edit (a renamed variable, a one-line import fix); the upper bound (1500/800) exists to avoid feeding an enormous vendored dump into the prompt, which would be expensive and not meaningfully "one diff" a user could answer questions about anyway.
./DECISIONS.md:45:**Alternatives considered:** No mechanical pre-filter at all — send every diff to the judge call and let it decide, including on trivial or huge diffs. Rejected because it burns a paid/rate-limited generation call on cases that are cheap to rule out mechanically first (a one-line formatting fix doesn't need an LLM to know it's not worth a question), which works against the cost-cap safety rail the brief treats as load-bearing. A more elaborate heuristic (e.g. weighting by file type or churn history) was also considered and rejected as exactly the kind of static-pattern complexity the brief already rejected for the "worth asking" decision generally — better to keep the mechanical layer dumb and push anything requiring real judgment into the one call built for it.
./DECISIONS.md:49:## 2026-08-04 — Cost cap default
./DECISIONS.md:51:**Decision:** $0.25 per session.
./DECISIONS.md:53:**Reasoning:** The brief suggests "a few cents per session" as a default without a number. $0.25 comfortably covers several judge+generate calls (a session with, say, 8 meaningful diffs) at the per-call cost a `--max-turns 1`, no-tools headless call is expected to run, while still being small enough that hitting it in a single session is a rare, noticeable event rather than a routine occurrence — which matters because the brief treats the cap being hit as a logged signal worth paying attention to, not just a silent ceiling.
./DECISIONS.md:55:**Alternatives considered:** A lower default (e.g. $0.05) was considered to hew closer to "a few cents," but rejected as likely to be hit routinely on a single long Claude Code session, which would make the cap noise rather than signal and undercut its purpose as a safety rail rather than a nuisance. A higher default (e.g. $1.00) was rejected as loose enough to stop functioning as a meaningful anxiety-reducing ceiling, which is the cap's actual job per §3.3.
./DECISIONS.md:59:## 2026-08-04 — Questions-per-session cap default
./DECISIONS.md:61:**Decision:** 8 questions per session.
./DECISIONS.md:63:**Reasoning:** The brief requires this cap (§5.2, §5.3, success criteria) but gives no number. 8 is enough to meaningfully scale content across a long unattended run (§5.3's stated goal) without producing the "unanswerable backlog" the same section explicitly warns against — a user returning to 8 queued questions is a lot but plausibly workable in one sitting; a user returning to 30+ is not.
./DECISIONS.md:65:**Alternatives considered:** A smaller cap (e.g. 3) was considered but rejected as working against §5.3's explicit point that a single question shouldn't have to represent an entire long wait — capping too low reintroduces the "one 20-second question doesn't fill a 10-minute wait" problem the brief calls out by name. An uncapped queue was rejected outright since the brief explicitly requires a cap to prevent an unanswerable backlog.
./DECISIONS.md:93:**Reasoning:** Manual hand-editing of Claude Code's hook config would reintroduce exactly the onboarding friction the brief works hard to eliminate elsewhere (§3.3 rejects BYOK partly on these grounds: "reintroduces exactly the onboarding friction the piggyback approach exists to eliminate"). Auto-install with a shown diff and explicit confirmation keeps install to effectively one command while still respecting that Grasp is silently intercepting the user's coding session and shipping diffs to an LLM — §3.5's "trust is load-bearing" stance means the user should see what's being changed in their own tool config, not have it happen invisibly.
./DECISIONS.md:99:## 2026-08-04 — Session definition for capping and batching
./DECISIONS.md:101:**Decision:** A "session" is one Claude Code invocation, bounded by the adapter's `onSessionComplete` (i.e., the `Stop` hook firing). This definition applies uniformly to both the cost cap (§3.3) and the question queue/batching (§5.3).
./DECISIONS.md:103:**Reasoning:** The brief already ties `onSessionComplete` to "flush queue, present before final output" in its own adapter interface sketch (§5.1), so a wall-clock or otherwise independently-defined "session" would require inventing a second boundary concept the brief never asks for, and risks the cost cap and the queue disagreeing about when a session starts/ends. Binding both to the same hook-driven boundary keeps the mental model single: one Claude Code run is one unit of spend and one unit of batched questions.
./DECISIONS.md:105:**Alternatives considered:** A wall-clock window (e.g. reset the cap/queue every N minutes of inactivity) was considered as a way to handle a user who runs several short back-to-back Claude Code invocations without ever really "leaving," but rejected as a speculative complexity the brief doesn't ask for — it would need its own idle-detection logic (which is explicitly the kind of presence-detection machinery the brief defers in Section 4's "presence-adaptive delivery" row), for a scenario that isn't described as a problem anywhere in the brief.
./DECISIONS.md:113:**Reasoning:** The brief is explicit and repeated on this point: v1 has exactly one generation path (§3.3, "No fallback tiers"), and every cost source is supposed to be visible through the cap the user configures. A second LLM call purely to summarize a diff for a log field would be an uncapped, unauthorized cost source running outside the one path the brief designed cost tracking around — it would also fire on every captured diff, including ones the Phase 4 filter or Phase 5 judge decide aren't worth a question, which is the opposite of "keep it cheap" (§3.3's framing for the one call that is allowed).
./DECISIONS.md:141:**Decision:** When a per-repo `.grasp.json` overrides a key that's an array (e.g. `ignorePatterns`) or any non-object value, the repo's value wholly replaces the global value. Only plain-object values (like `diffThresholds`) merge key-by-key; arrays and primitives never merge.
./DECISIONS.md:143:**Reasoning:** Array concatenation is the more surprising behavior for a config override — a user writing `"ignorePatterns": ["vendor/"]` in a repo almost certainly means "add this on top of whatever's global," which concatenation would give them, but concatenation also makes it impossible to *remove* a global pattern for one repo, and silently duplicates entries if the same override file is ever loaded twice. Replace-on-conflict is simpler to reason about and matches how most JSON config-merge tools (e.g. ESLint's `overrides`, ts-node's compiler options) behave by default.
./DECISIONS.md:145:**Alternatives considered:** Concatenate arrays (union of global + repo values) — appealing for `ignorePatterns` specifically (purely additive use case) but wrong for a hypothetical future array-valued setting where a repo genuinely wants to override rather than extend, and inconsistent to special-case by key name. Deep-merge arrays by index — clearly wrong for a list of independent string patterns; rejected without much consideration.
./DECISIONS.md:153:**Reasoning:** Phase 1 has no git/repo-detection logic yet — that machinery doesn't exist until the capture adapters in Phases 2–3 — so implementing upward search now would mean guessing at how "repo root" gets defined before the phase that actually owns that definition has been built. Resolving against the exact directory Grasp is invoked from is also simplest to reason about and test today, and does not foreclose adding upward search later: Phase 2/3 can pass whatever directory they determine to be the repo root into this same `loadConfig(repoRoot)` signature without changing this function at all.
./DECISIONS.md:155:**Alternatives considered:** Walk up from `cwd` to the nearest `.git` directory, `.grasp.json`, or filesystem root (the common pattern for tools like ESLint/Prettier) — more convenient for a user running `grasp` from a subdirectory, but requires deciding *now* what "repo root" means, which duplicates work the capture-adapter phases will need to do anyway with actual git integration. Deferred rather than rejected outright — likely to be revisited once Phase 2/3 need a real repo-root concept.
./DECISIONS.md:173:**Reasoning:** Three flat, mutually-exclusive commands with no options/flags of their own don't justify a parsing library; a manual switch is fewer lines and one fewer dependency to audit for an open-source tool where §3.6 already treats trust as load-bearing. This isn't meant to hold for later phases — once real commands need flags, subcommands, and `--help` text generated per-command (Phase 7+, once the TUI and gate config surface grow), a proper CLI framework is likely worth revisiting.
./DECISIONS.md:189:## 2026-08-04 — Git-diff capture: comparison mechanism (diff against HEAD, not the index or a stored snapshot)
./DECISIONS.md:191:**Decision:** `captureGitDiff(repoPath)` diffs the working tree against `HEAD` (or, if the repo has no commits yet, against git's empty-tree object `4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904`) using `git diff -M <baseRef>`, then separately unions in untracked files (via `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard`, each diffed with `git diff --no-index -- /dev/null <file>`). "No changes" is simply `files.length === 0` on the resulting object — there's no separate empty-repo special case to detect.
./DECISIONS.md:193:**Reasoning:** Brief §5.1 only loosely specifies this ("watch git working-tree state / `git diff` between checkpoints"), leaving the actual comparison base open. Diffing against `HEAD` captures everything not yet committed — staged and unstaged changes to tracked files alike — which is the right proxy for "what the agent just did" in a coding-agent workflow, since agents essentially never run `git add`/`git commit` themselves; whatever's uncommitted *is* the work in progress. `HEAD` also requires no state of Grasp's own: a single invocation is self-sufficient, with no prior "checkpoint" to have remembered. Untracked files need separate handling because `git diff <ref>` never includes them by design; `git diff --no-index` against `/dev/null` produces a correctly-formatted unified diff for a new file's full content without touching the index (kept read-only throughout — no `git add -N` or similar mutation).
./DECISIONS.md:195:**Alternatives considered:** Diff against the index only (`git diff`, no ref) — simplest, and what the brief's own example command shows, but misses any changes an agent (or the user) happens to have staged, which a pure "diff HEAD" comparison doesn't; rejected as strictly less complete for the same complexity. A stateful "diff between checkpoints" — store a snapshot (tree hash, or full file contents) at the end of each capture and diff against it next time, matching the brief's literal "between checkpoints" phrasing more closely — was considered and set aside for this phase specifically because there's no poll loop or persistent capture-daemon yet for "checkpoints" to mean anything; `HEAD` is the closest stateless proxy available today, and a real checkpoint concept is more naturally Phase 3's problem once hooks define actual event boundaries (`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop`) to checkpoint against.
./DECISIONS.md:203:**Reasoning:** `execFile` (not `exec`) passes arguments as an array rather than a shell string, so repo paths or filenames with spaces/special characters can't be misinterpreted as shell syntax — the safety property that matters without needing a library for it. Beyond that, the actual value a library like `simple-git` provides is a parsed, opinionated view of git's output; this phase needs the opposite — exact control over flags (`--no-color`, `-M`, `--numstat`, `--name-status`) and access to git's raw unified-diff text so Grasp can parse it into its own `CapturedDiff` shape, which a library's own parsed diff objects would just have to be unwound from anyway. A thin wrapper is also what Phase 3 needs regardless: Claude Code's hooks are themselves just shell commands invoking a Node script, so the same `runGit`/`captureGitDiff` functions are directly importable there with no adapter-specific glue — a library dependency wouldn't make that reuse any easier, and would add one more piece of surface area for an open-source tool where trust is explicitly load-bearing (brief §3.6).
./DECISIONS.md:205:**Alternatives considered:** `simple-git` — mature, promise-based, handles a lot of git plumbing conveniently, but its parsed output for `diff`/`log` doesn't map cleanly onto the hunk-level structure `CapturedDiff` needs, meaning raw-output access would still be required underneath, making the library mostly redundant overhead. `isomorphic-git` — a pure-JS git implementation, valuable when a real git binary isn't guaranteed to exist (browser, constrained sandboxes); irrelevant here since anyone using an AI coding agent against a git repo already has git installed, and the fallback capture path's own reason for existing (§5.1) is to work with *any* agent, not to work without git.
./DECISIONS.md:221:**Decision:** `ClaudeCodeHookPayload` (in `src/adapters/claudeCodeAdapter.ts`) is typed from Anthropic's current official hooks reference (`https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks`, fetched live during this session), not from memory/assumption and not from an actual observed hook firing. `session_id`, `cwd`, and `hook_event_name` are treated as reliably present on every event (the docs list them as common fields present across all hook types, with concrete matching examples for `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop`). `prompt_id` is treated as *unconfirmed* — the docs note it's "absent until first user input," which is ambiguous about whether it's reliably present by the time `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop` can fire — so the code never assumes it's set (see `resolvePromptId`'s fallback, logged separately below).
./DECISIONS.md:223:**Reasoning:** The task asked for genuine empirical verification — wiring a scratch stdin-dumping hook into a real Claude Code session — the same discipline used for git output formats in Phase 2. That was attempted properly, not skipped: a probe hook (`/private/tmp/grasp-hook-probe/dump.js`) was wired into this live session's own project `.claude/settings.json` first (didn't fire — hooks appear to load once at session start, not live-reloaded mid-session, so a hook added after the session began never took effect for the rest of it); then a fresh, separate Claude Code process was attempted two ways — `npx @anthropic-ai/claude-code` (a genuinely separate install) and the exact `claude` binary bundled with the desktop app at `$CLAUDE_CODE_EXECPATH` (same binary family driving this very session). Both reported `"Not logged in · Please run /login"` / `{"loggedIn": false}` — this sandboxed environment has no standalone CLI-level authentication a subprocess can use, distinct from whatever protocol the desktop app uses to drive this session directly. Forcing a `/login` OAuth flow or hunting for credentials elsewhere on the machine was judged out of bounds (not what was asked, and risks using the user's account for a side purpose without being asked). Given a true live-fire test wasn't achievable in this environment, live official documentation (fetched fresh, not recalled) is the next-most-reliable source — better than proceeding on the brief's high-level description or unverified prior assumption, but still one step short of the actual empirical check the task asked for.
./DECISIONS.md:225:**Alternatives considered:** Proceed on the brief's own high-level description alone (`PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `Stop` "are the natural triggers") — rejected outright per the task's explicit instruction not to. Skip the payload-shape question and design the adapter generically enough not to need to know it — not viable, since the whole architectural problem (how to correlate separate hook-invocation processes into one logical unit) hinges on exactly which correlating fields (`session_id`, `prompt_id`) actually exist on the payload. **Flagged for follow-up:** whoever next has a real authenticated Claude Code CLI available should run the scratch probe (left in place conceptually — see `checkForChanges`-style verification pattern used elsewhere) against a real interactive session and confirm `prompt_id`'s actual behavior before this ships to real users.
./DECISIONS.md:229:## 2026-08-04 — Stop fires once per conversational turn, not once per whole CLI session
./DECISIONS.md:231:**Decision:** `ClaudeCodeAdapter`'s unit of work is one *turn* — the pair `(session_id, prompt_id)` — not one whole interactive Claude Code session. `onSessionComplete()` still fires from the `Stop` hook, unchanged from the already-shipped Phase 1/2 design, but the earlier "Session definition for capping and batching" entry's description of `Stop` as bounding "one Claude Code invocation" is corrected here: that's only true for headless (`-p`) invocations, where one turn *is* the whole invocation. In a real interactive session, `Stop` fires after every turn — potentially many times across one long interactive Claude Code session.
./DECISIONS.md:233:**Reasoning:** A second doc fetch, asked specifically to resolve this ("does Stop fire once per turn or once per whole session? Is there a separate SessionEnd?"), confirmed: `Stop` is listed explicitly as a **once-per-turn** event, fired every time Claude finishes responding to a single prompt — and there's a *separate* `SessionEnd` event ("session terminates") that BUILD_PLAN.md's Phase 3 scope never asked for and this phase doesn't wire up. This actually turns out to still be the *right* binding for `onSessionComplete`, not a bug to route around: the `AgentAdapter` interface's own annotation for this method is "flush queue, present before final output" (brief §5.1) — "final output" most naturally reads as *this turn's* output, i.e. exactly the point where Grasp should interject with a question before the user sees what Claude just did. That's a per-turn gate, which is what `Stop` actually provides. So Phase 3's `Stop`→`onSessionComplete()` wiring is correct as built; what was wrong was the informal shorthand "session = one Claude Code invocation" in the earlier decision, which reads naturally as "the whole time the user has `claude` open," and isn't accurate for interactive use.
./DECISIONS.md:235:**Consequence flagged for Phase 5/8, not resolved here:** the cost cap (§3.3, $0.25 default) and questions-per-session cap (§5.2/§5.3, 8 default) were sized assuming a broader "session" than one turn — a $0.25 cap or an 8-question cap that fully resets on every single conversational turn would barely constrain a long interactive work session (many turns) at all, which likely isn't what those defaults were meant to protect against. Phase 5/8 will need to explicitly decide whether spend/question caps key off `(session_id, prompt_id)` (matches this phase's turn granularity, resets every turn) or off `session_id` alone (spans the whole interactive session, requiring `SessionStart`/`SessionEnd` — hooks not wired in this phase) or something else. Not decided here — out of Phase 3's scope (capture-and-install only) — but recorded so it isn't silently inherited as settled.
./DECISIONS.md:237:**Alternatives considered:** Wire `SessionStart`/`SessionEnd` now instead of/in addition to `Stop`, to get a true whole-session boundary — rejected for this phase because BUILD_PLAN.md's Phase 3 scope, and the task's own instructions for this session, explicitly name only `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop`; adding a fourth hook family (with its own untested payload shape) is exactly the kind of scope growth the phase boundary exists to prevent, and the cap-boundary question it would answer belongs to Phase 5/8 anyway. Silently keep calling it "session" and hope the ambiguity doesn't matter — rejected because it's exactly the kind of gap that would surface confusingly later (Phase 5/8 quietly building on a wrong mental model) rather than now, when it's cheap to flag.
./DECISIONS.md:241:## 2026-08-04 — ClaudeCodeAdapter: session-state persistence across hook invocations
./DECISIONS.md:243:**Decision:** Cross-invocation state lives in three new SQLite tables (`cc_turns`, `captured_diffs`, `hook_invocations`), keyed by Claude Code's own `(session_id, prompt_id)` pair — not a temp file, not an in-memory daemon, and not additions to the existing Phase 1 `events` table. `cc_turns` tracks one row per turn (`started_at`, nullable `completed_at`); `completeTurn()` is an atomic, idempotent `UPDATE ... WHERE completed_at IS NULL` so a redundant `Stop` firing for an already-completed turn safely no-ops. `captured_diffs` holds the diffs a turn's `PostToolUse` firings actually captured (the adapter's real output). `hook_invocations` is a pure append-only audit trail of every hook firing observed, which is what makes "did `Stop` really fire exactly once for this turn" independently verifiable from the DB rather than trusted on faith.
./DECISIONS.md:245:**Reasoning:** Each hook firing is a separate, short-lived Node process with no shared memory with any other firing — the whole architectural problem this decision exists to solve. Three options were weighed as the task asked: (1) using Claude Code's own `session_id`/`prompt_id` as the correlating key, vs. Grasp inventing its own session-boundary heuristic; (2) SQLite vs. a temp file for where state lives between firings; (3) how this reconciles with the existing `events` table and the (now-corrected) "session" concept. On (1): Claude Code's own identifiers are authoritative, already correctly scoped by the tool that owns the actual session lifecycle, and (per the entry above) `prompt_id` gives exactly the per-turn granularity `onSessionComplete` semantically needs — Grasp has no reason to invent a parallel concept when a correct one is handed to it on every hook call. On (2): Phase 1 already put the SQLite store in WAL mode specifically anticipating "a hook-triggered background process potentially writing events while [something else] reads the same file" — this scenario was foreseen, not new; a temp-file scheme would duplicate state better expressed in the store Grasp already has, need its own hand-rolled atomicity/locking story, risk orphaned files if `Stop` never fires (session killed mid-task — verification step 3), and wouldn't show up if a user inspects `~/.grasp/history.db` directly, undermining §3.5's "plain and inspectable" goal. On (3): a new `captured_diffs` table, not new columns on `events`, because `events` rows represent question/miss events under brief §3.5's own schema (question text, type, answer, cost) — a raw capture that hasn't yet been judged "worth a question" (Phase 4/5's job) isn't that, and giving it its own table keeps `events`' existing meaning intact rather than growing it a dual purpose. `captured_diffs`/`cc_turns` do **not** touch the existing `events` table at all, so no migration of the Phase 1 schema was needed this phase — flagged for whenever Phase 5/6 starts writing real `events` rows and needs to correlate them back to a session, which will need its own `session_id` column added then (not now, since nothing writes to `events` in Phase 3).
./DECISIONS.md:247:**Alternatives considered:** A temp file per session (e.g. `/tmp/grasp-session-<id>.json`) — simplest to picture, but loses concurrent-safety and inspectability for no offsetting benefit given SQLite/WAL was already the answer to this exact problem shape. Grasp generating its own session/turn identifiers (e.g. a UUID minted on first hook firing seen) instead of using Claude Code's `session_id`/`prompt_id` — rejected as pure duplicated complexity: Claude Code already hands over a correct, authoritative identifier on every call, so inventing a second one would only risk drifting out of sync with the thing it's trying to describe. Storing captured diffs as new nullable columns directly on `events` — rejected per the schema-purity reasoning above.
./DECISIONS.md:251:## 2026-08-04 — Hook config location: project-level `.claude/settings.local.json`, not user-level or committed
./DECISIONS.md:253:**Decision:** `grasp init` writes to `<repo>/.claude/settings.local.json` — project-scoped and local-only — not `~/.claude/settings.json` (applies to every project) and not the shareable/committed `<repo>/.claude/settings.json`.
./DECISIONS.md:255:**Reasoning:** Per the current docs (`code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks`), all three locations are valid places Claude Code reads hook config from, so this was a genuine open choice, verified rather than assumed. User-level `~/.claude/settings.json` would mean running `grasp init` once ever silently enables Grasp's hooks for *every* Claude Code project the user ever opens, including ones they never asked to be monitored — that doesn't fit the per-repo opt-in mental model the task's own verification steps assume ("run `grasp init` in a scratch repo," "run it a second time... in that repo"), and conflicts with "teach, don't punish": a tool that silently follows you into unrelated projects is closer to the punishment-tool failure mode the brief explicitly warns against (§3.1). The committed, shareable `.claude/settings.json` is worse in a different way: since it's meant to be checked into git, it would silently enable Grasp's hooks for every teammate who clones the repo the moment one person runs `grasp init` and commits the result — bypassing the individual, explicit confirmation the "Hook registration mechanics" decision already requires *per user*. `settings.local.json` is documented as gitignored by convention specifically for local-only overrides, which is exactly Grasp's use case: one user, one machine, opted in themselves.
./DECISIONS.md:257:**Alternatives considered:** `~/.claude/settings.json` (user-level, all projects) — rejected per the "silently follows you everywhere" reasoning above. Committed `.claude/settings.json` (project-level, shared) — appealing for teams that want everyone using Grasp on a shared repo, but rejected for v1 because it breaks the per-user consent model already committed to; worth reconsidering later as an explicit opt-in "team mode" if real usage ever asks for it, but not a v1 default.
./DECISIONS.md:261:## 2026-08-04 — Missing `prompt_id` fallback: collapse to session-wide, don't fragment
./DECISIONS.md:263:**Decision:** `resolvePromptId()` (`src/adapters/claudeCodeAdapter.ts`) falls back to a single fixed sentinel key (`"__no_prompt_id__"`) whenever a hook payload's `prompt_id` is missing or empty — never a freshly-generated random id per invocation.
./DECISIONS.md:265:**Reasoning:** Given real uncertainty about whether `prompt_id` is reliably present (see the payload-shape entry above), the fallback needed to fail in a *safe* direction. A fixed sentinel means every hook firing within a session that lacks `prompt_id` collapses onto the *same* `(session_id, "__no_prompt_id__")` turn row — degrading gracefully to exactly the coarser "one turn per whole session_id" behavior the original, pre-verification Phase 1 assumption described. A random id per invocation would do the opposite: silently fragment a single real turn into many fake ones, each triggering its own `onSessionComplete()` — actively breaking the "exactly once, not once per tool call" guarantee the task explicitly asks to preserve, in exactly the failure mode being tested for.
./DECISIONS.md:273:**Decision:** `ClaudeCodeAdapter.checkAndCapture()` runs `captureGitDiff` on *every* `PostToolUse` firing, regardless of which tool ran (`Read`, `Grep`, `Bash`, `Edit`, ...) — there's no allowlist of "file-mutating tool names" gating whether a capture is attempted.
./DECISIONS.md:275:**Reasoning:** Building a tool-name allowlist (e.g. only capture after `Edit`/`Write`/`MultiEdit`/`NotebookEdit`, or `Bash` since it can run arbitrary file-mutating commands) would itself be a small filtering system — exactly the kind of scope Phase 3 ("capture-and-install only") isn't supposed to include; that judgment belongs with Phase 4. Running capture unconditionally and relying on Phase 2's already-correct empty-diff handling (`checkForChanges`'s `if (diff.files.length > 0)` pattern, reused as-is here) keeps Phase 3 honest about its own scope: a `Read`-only turn simply produces an empty diff and nothing gets persisted, at the cost of one harmless extra `git diff` subprocess call.
./DECISIONS.md:277:**Alternatives considered:** Allowlist a small set of known file-mutating tool names to skip capture on obviously-read-only tools (`Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`, `WebFetch`, ...) — would save a subprocess call on those turns, but requires maintaining a list of tool names that could silently go stale as Claude Code adds tools, and is itself a filtering judgment call better made once, deliberately, in Phase 4 rather than informally re-invented here.
./DECISIONS.md:307:**Alternatives considered:** A real glob matcher (`minimatch`) — more powerful and more familiar to anyone who's used `.gitignore`, but adds a dependency for capability nothing in scope currently needs, and glob semantics (especially `**` vs `*`, negation with `!`) have enough edge cases to be worth deferring until a real user actually asks for a pattern the simple matcher can't express. Plain substring matching (pattern matches if it appears anywhere in the path string) — rejected as too loose: a user pattern `"test"` would then also match `"latest.js"` or `"contest/"`, which is a real false-positive risk a basename/segment match doesn't have.
./DECISIONS.md:311:## 2026-08-04 — Filtered-diff recording: extend `captured_diffs`, don't add a new table or touch `events`
./DECISIONS.md:313:**Decision:** `captured_diffs` (introduced in Phase 3) gets two new columns — `filtered INTEGER NOT NULL DEFAULT 0` and `filter_reason TEXT` (nullable) — rather than a new table, and `events` is not touched at all. Every capture gets exactly one `captured_diffs` row regardless of the filter's verdict; a filtered-out diff is `filtered=1, filter_reason=<one of the FilterReason values>`, a passed one is `filtered=0, filter_reason=NULL`. The full original `diff_json` is stored either way — filtering never truncates or rewrites what was actually captured, only annotates it.
./DECISIONS.md:315:**Reasoning:** This directly continues the reasoning already logged in Phase 3's "session-state persistence" entry for why `captured_diffs` exists separately from `events` in the first place: `events` rows represent question/miss *events* under brief §3.5's schema (question text, type, answer, cost) — a mechanically-filtered-out diff was never even considered for a question, so it isn't that, any more than an unfiltered capture was. `captured_diffs`, by contrast, was already defined as "diffs Grasp captured, pending whatever Phase 4/5 eventually does with them" — Phase 4's filter verdict *is* exactly that "what happens next" judgment for the mechanical half, so annotating the existing row is the natural continuation of the table's own stated purpose, not scope creep into it. A new table (e.g. `filtered_diffs`) would just split one capture's lifecycle across two tables for no benefit — every filtered diff is still fundamentally "a `captured_diffs` row," just one that didn't survive the mechanical gate. Storing the full diff regardless of verdict (rather than, say, only storing a summary for filtered-out ones) matches brief §3.5's "local-first, inspectable" stance: a user should be able to look at *exactly* what got filtered and why, not a redacted version of it.
./DECISIONS.md:317:**Alternatives considered:** A separate `filtered_diffs` table — considered and rejected per the "splits one capture's lifecycle in two" reasoning above; it would also need its own FK/index setup duplicating `captured_diffs`' existing shape for no real gain. Reusing `events` with `question_concept`/`question_instance` left `NULL` and a new `miss_reason`-like value for "filtered" — rejected because `events.miss_reason` already has a specific, narrower meaning under brief §3.5 (`error`/`timeout`/`cap_reached` — a *generation* call that was attempted and failed, per §3.3), and overloading it to also mean "never attempted because mechanically filtered" would blur two genuinely different situations behind one field. Silently dropping filtered diffs with no record at all — rejected outright per the task's explicit instruction and brief §3.5's inspectability stance.
./DECISIONS.md:325:**Reasoning:** The task's own "Integration point" framing is specifically about how a filtered diff gets recorded in `captured_diffs` — a table that only exists in a meaningful form for the Claude-Code-hook pipeline, because it's keyed by `(session_id, prompt_id)` with a foreign key into `cc_turns`, both introduced in Phase 3 specifically to solve the hook-invocation session-state problem. `GitDiffAdapter` has no session/turn concept at all (it's a manual/poll-based capture — Phase 2's own scope was "produce a diff object," verified via CLI stdout, never persistence), so wiring it into `captured_diffs` would first require inventing a synthetic session/turn identity for something that isn't hook-driven — a real design question of its own that neither Phase 2 nor this phase's task was asked to solve. Keeping the filter function itself adapter-agnostic (no `db`, no session/turn parameters, just `diff` + `config` in, a verdict out) means this isn't a wall — whenever `GitDiffAdapter` (or a future adapter) gets its own persistence story, it calls the exact same `evaluateCapturedDiff`, not a rewritten one.
./DECISIONS.md:327:**Alternatives considered:** Give `GitDiffAdapter` a `db` handle and invent a synthetic session/turn key (e.g. derived from a timestamp or a poll-cycle counter) so it can also write filtered `captured_diffs` rows — rejected as solving an out-of-scope problem (what does "a session" mean for manual/poll-based capture?) as a side effect of this phase, when the task scoped the integration point specifically around the hook pipeline's existing table. Make `captured_diffs`' `session_id`/`prompt_id` columns nullable to accommodate non-hook-driven captures — rejected because it would weaken the FK relationship to `cc_turns` that Phase 3 deliberately established, for a caller that doesn't exist yet.
./DECISIONS.md:331:## 2026-08-04 — Resolving Phase 3's flagged consequence: cap accounting keys off `session_id` alone, not `(session_id, prompt_id)`
./DECISIONS.md:333:**Decision:** This directly resolves the "Consequence flagged for Phase 5/8, not resolved here" paragraph in the "Stop fires once per conversational turn, not once per whole CLI session" entry above. The resolution: the cost cap (`costCapUsd`) accumulates by summing `events.cost_usd` across **every row for the current `session_id`**, regardless of which `prompt_id` (turn) produced it — not scoped to `(session_id, prompt_id)`. `Stop`'s existing per-turn firing (unchanged from Phase 3 — still the trigger for `onSessionComplete`, still fires once per turn) is untouched; only the *accounting boundary* for spend needed correcting, not the presentation-trigger boundary. No new hook (`SessionStart`/`SessionEnd`) is required: `session_id` is already present on every `PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop` payload Phase 3 already observed and already stores on every `cc_turns`/`captured_diffs`/`hook_invocations` row — the cumulative-per-`session_id` query Phase 5 needs is `SELECT SUM(cost_usd) FROM events WHERE session_id = ?`, nothing more.
./DECISIONS.md:335:**Reasoning:** Phase 3's flagged entry laid out the fork plainly: key the cap off `(session_id, prompt_id)` (matches `Stop`'s own per-turn granularity, resets every turn) or off `session_id` alone (spans the whole interactive sitting). The flagged entry also already explained *why* per-turn accounting doesn't serve the cap's actual purpose: "a $0.25 cap or an 8-question cap that fully resets on every single conversational turn would barely constrain a long interactive work session (many turns) at all." A user working through a long multi-turn Claude Code session — the exact scenario the cap exists to protect against runaway spend in — would see their nominal $0.25 cap actually mean "$0.25 × however many turns they submit," which could be dollars of real spend with no effective ceiling, defeating the cap's entire stated purpose in brief §3.3 ("a few cents per session," treated as a hard safety rail, not a per-message allowance). Crucially, this doesn't require a new hook: `session_id` was already flowing through the whole pipeline before this phase — Phase 3's hook payload already carries it on every firing, and Phase 3's own tables already key rows by it (alongside `prompt_id` for the finer turn-level correlation those tables specifically needed). The only thing missing was a place to *read* `session_id` back off of stored `events` rows for the cumulative-sum query — which is exactly what adding the `events.session_id` column (this phase, item 3) provides. `Stop` continues to fire per-turn and continues to be what triggers presenting whatever's ready (once Phase 7/8 build presentation) — that trigger-timing question and the spend-accounting-boundary question are genuinely separate, and only the latter needed correcting.
./DECISIONS.md:337:**Alternatives considered:** Key the cap off `(session_id, prompt_id)` — rejected per the reasoning above; this is the option Phase 3's flagged entry itself already identified as the one that "wouldn't serve the cap's purpose." Introduce `SessionStart`/`SessionEnd` hooks to get an explicit whole-session boundary distinct from `session_id` alone — unnecessary: `session_id` already uniquely identifies one interactive sitting (or one headless invocation) on its own, per the hook payload's own documented semantics (Phase 3's payload-shape entry); a session-scoped `SUM(...) WHERE session_id = ?` query gets the identical boundary without adding a fourth hook family, its own untested payload shape, and the install/wiring cost that would come with it. A wall-clock or idle-timeout-based session boundary — already rejected once, in the original "Session definition for capping and batching" entry, for inventing presence-detection machinery the brief explicitly defers (Section 4); nothing about resolving this flagged item changes that reasoning.
./DECISIONS.md:347:**Alternatives considered:** Let the model decide the concept-question inclusion entirely on its own, with no after-the-fact enforcement — closer to a literal one-shot reading of the brief, but rejected because it makes a stated correctness guarantee (§3.2) contingent on model reliability rather than Grasp's own data, for zero savings (the lookup is already needed for the prompt's history list, or trivially cheap either way). Two separate calls — a judge call, then a conditional generate call only if worth asking — rejected because the brief explicitly frames this as "one smarter call, not two calls," and a second call would double the cost-cap exposure per diff for no benefit the single-call design doesn't already get. Ask the model to return an array of concept tags instead of one — rejected as unnecessary complexity for what the brief itself describes as one concept a diff exercises, and it would turn the memoization check into a multi-tag join instead of a single lookup.
./DECISIONS.md:353:**Decision:** Every failure mode in the generation call — the `claude` process failing to spawn or exit cleanly, its stdout not parsing as JSON at all, the outer envelope's `is_error: true`, or the inner `result` text not matching the judge/generate contract (`parseJudgeResponse` returning `null`) — is caught and recorded as a single `events` row with `miss_reason: "error"`, never thrown, never left unlogged. The one exception is the cap-refusal path, which is its own `miss_reason: "cap_reached"` and never invokes the process at all. No timeout is added around the `claude -p` subprocess call itself in this phase. Whenever an envelope was actually obtained from the process (even one that's `is_error: true` or whose `result` doesn't parse), its `total_cost_usd` is still recorded on the miss row; only when the process/outer-JSON step itself fails outright is `cost_usd` left `null` (genuinely unknown, not assumed to be 0).
./DECISIONS.md:355:**Reasoning:** The task is explicit that full retry/timeout/error-taxonomy work is Phase 6's job, but "some baseline 'don't crash on a malformed response' handling belongs here since you can't ship generation code that assumes a perfect response" — so Phase 5's bar is specifically "never crash, never leave a real attempt unlogged," not "correctly classify every failure mode," which is why every distinct failure collapses to the single existing `error` value from brief §3.3's own vocabulary (`error`/`timeout`/`cap_reached`) rather than inventing new ones Phase 6 would then have to reconcile. Recording cost whenever it's known, even on a miss, closes a real gap: without it, a model that returns a well-formed `is_error:false` envelope but garbage `result` text would let a user's real spend climb without the cap ever seeing it (since the cap sums `events.cost_usd`, and a call that "doesn't count" because it failed to parse is still a call the user was actually charged for) — recording it is what keeps the cap honest against exactly the failure mode most likely to repeat if a model is having a bad day. Not adding a timeout here is a deliberate boundary respected, not an oversight: BUILD_PLAN.md's Phase 6 explicitly lists "a defensive timeout on the subprocess call itself" as its own deliverable, and the task for this session says not to build Phase 6 scope beyond the minimal cap-refusal path — so an unbounded (at this layer) subprocess call is the correct, intentional gap for this phase, left for Phase 6 to close.
./DECISIONS.md:357:**Alternatives considered:** Distinguish parse failures from process failures from `is_error` failures with different `miss_reason` values now — rejected as pre-empting Phase 6's own error-taxonomy work with a scheme this phase has no way to validate is the right one (e.g. whether `timeout` should be its own detected case here at all is exactly Phase 6's question to answer, once a real timeout mechanism exists to detect it). Treat malformed/`is_error` responses as `cost_usd: null` (matching the "genuinely unknown" treatment of a total process failure) — rejected because the envelope *did* report a number in that case; discarding a known figure is strictly worse for cap correctness than recording it, even though the question itself was lost. Let a malformed response propagate as a thrown exception up to the hook entrypoint's own outer catch (which already exists per Phase 3's "never crash" hook design) instead of handling it inside `runGeneration` — rejected because that path would skip writing an `events` row entirely, silently losing the record of the attempt (and its cost) rather than degrading gracefully to a logged miss, which is the whole point of brief §3.3's miss-logging requirement.
./DECISIONS.md:361:## 2026-08-04 — Bumped the Phase 3 hook timeout (15s → 45s) to fit a real generation call
./DECISIONS.md:363:**Decision:** `grasp init`'s installed hook `timeout` (in `src/init.ts`) is now 45 seconds, up from Phase 3's original 15.
./DECISIONS.md:365:**Reasoning:** Phase 3 picked 15s before generation existed, for hooks that only did local git/SQLite work. `PostToolUse` now runs Phase 5's synchronous `claude -p --max-turns 1` call inline (see the `ClaudeCodeAdapter.onChangeDetected` wiring), which can plausibly take longer than 15s depending on model load — and Claude Code killing the hook mid-call would abort generation with no `events` row written at all (worse than any of the miss paths above, since even those guarantee a row). This is not Phase 6's "defensive timeout on the subprocess call itself" (that's an inner timeout Grasp would set on its own `execFileSync` call, still unbuilt) — it's just widening an outer ceiling that already existed so it doesn't undercut work this phase adds inside it.
./DECISIONS.md:367:**Alternatives considered:** Leave it at 15s and treat any resulting hook-level kill as Phase 6's problem to eventually catch — rejected because an outer hook timeout killing the process happens *outside* any of Grasp's own try/catch blocks, so nothing Phase 6 builds inside `runGeneration` could ever catch or log it; the outer ceiling has to be wide enough for Phase 5's own happy path to plausibly finish, independent of what Phase 6 later adds. Add Phase 6's inner subprocess timeout now instead of bumping the outer one — rejected as out of this session's explicit scope; a numeric config default is a narrow, self-contained fix that doesn't require building the subsystem the task says not to build yet. Note: existing installs that already ran `grasp init` keep their old 15s value until they delete Grasp's entries and re-run `grasp init` — `grasp init`'s idempotency check only looks at whether the command string is present, not its timeout value (a pre-existing Phase 3 limitation, not something this phase's narrow fix attempts to solve).
./DECISIONS.md:371:## 2026-08-04 — Generation subprocess timeout value: 20 seconds
./DECISIONS.md:373:**Decision:** `invokeClaudeJudge`'s `execFileSync` call now sets `timeout: 20_000` (ms) — `GENERATION_TIMEOUT_MS` in `src/generation.ts`.
./DECISIONS.md:375:**Reasoning:** The real constraint is the Phase 5 outer Claude Code hook timeout (45s, `src/init.ts`), inside which the whole `PostToolUse` handler — including this subprocess call *and* Grasp's own cleanup (catching the error, writing the miss row) — has to finish, or the hook process gets killed outright with no `events` row written at all (strictly worse than any miss path, since even a logged miss guarantees a row). 20s leaves a 25-second gap to the 45s ceiling — larger than the timeout value itself — for: (a) Node's own kill-and-throw sequence once the timeout elapses (empirically near-instant, confirmed while testing this phase — see the "timeout detection mechanism" entry below for the same test), (b) `recordMiss`'s single synchronous `better-sqlite3` insert (a local, WAL-mode, single-row write — consistently sub-millisecond in this project's own testing), and (c) normal process/hook wrap-up overhead. That 25s margin is generous by roughly 2 orders of magnitude versus what (a)+(b)+(c) actually need in practice, which is deliberate: the goal isn't the tightest safe value, it's a value where even a heavily loaded or slow machine has no realistic way to blow through the outer ceiling before the miss row is written. Separately, 20s is also a reasonable ceiling for the call's own *legitimate* happy path: a `--max-turns 1`, no-tools judge call reading one diff should typically finish in a few seconds; a call still running at 20s is behaving abnormally regardless of the outer-timeout math, and continuing to wait past that doesn't serve the user.
./DECISIONS.md:377:**Alternatives considered:** A tighter inner timeout (e.g. 10s) — would still work margin-wise, but risks false-timing-out legitimately slow-but-working calls (model under load, larger diffs, network variance) more than the extra margin buys in safety, given 20s vs 45s is already comfortably safe. A looser inner timeout (e.g. 35-40s) — technically still "less than 45," but rejected as cutting the margin too close: it would leave only 5-10s for the kill-and-cleanup sequence, which is still probably enough in practice but removes the deliberate, generous safety factor that protects against exactly the kind of transient slowness (disk contention, GC pause, a loaded dev machine) the margin exists for. Matching the two timeouts more tightly by formula (e.g. inner = outer − 5s) instead of picking an independent round number — considered, but the outer timeout isn't configurable either right now (it's `init.ts`'s own hardcoded constant), so there's no live value to derive from yet; revisit if the outer timeout ever becomes configurable.
./DECISIONS.md:381:## 2026-08-04 — Generation timeout: hardcoded constant, not a `GraspConfig` field
./DECISIONS.md:385:**Reasoning:** Every existing configurable threshold (`costCapUsd`, `diffThresholds`, `ignorePatterns`, `questionsPerSessionCap`) is a self-contained product/behavior preference — a user can change any one of them in isolation and get exactly the behavior they'd expect, with no coupling to some other value they'd also need to know about. The generation timeout isn't like that: its entire correctness depends on staying safely under the *outer* Claude Code hook timeout (45s, `src/init.ts`), which is itself currently hardcoded and not exposed anywhere the user would see it alongside this one. Exposing the inner timeout as a config knob would let a user set e.g. `generationTimeoutMs: 40000` — perfectly reasonable-looking in isolation — and silently reintroduce the exact "hook kills the process before the miss row can be written" failure this phase exists to close, with no way for the user to know the two numbers were ever related. That's a worse outcome than not offering the knob at all: a setting that looks safe to tune but quietly breaks a guarantee the user has no visibility into.
./DECISIONS.md:387:**Alternatives considered:** Add it to `GraspConfig` for consistency with every other threshold — rejected per the coupling risk above; consistency with the *shape* of other config isn't worth it when this value's safety, unlike the others, depends on a relationship with a second number the user can't see or edit. Add it to `GraspConfig` *and* validate at load time that it's safely less than the outer hook timeout — more defensible, but the outer timeout lives in `init.ts` and is only written to a per-repo `.claude/settings.local.json` at install time, not read back by the config loader anywhere; building that cross-check would be real new plumbing for a knob nothing in this session's scope asked for. Revisit if a real user actually hits legitimately-slow-but-working generation calls that 20s cuts off — that's the concrete signal (per the project's own standing preference for evidence over speculation) that would justify building the knob properly, together with making the outer timeout configurable in tandem rather than in isolation.
./DECISIONS.md:393:**Decision:** A generation-call failure is classified as `miss_reason: "timeout"` specifically when the caught error's `code` property is the string `"ETIMEDOUT"` (`isTimeoutError` in `src/generation.ts`). Every other failure — including ones where `error.signal === "SIGTERM"` — falls through to the existing `"error"` classification.
./DECISIONS.md:395:**Reasoning:** The task explicitly named `error.killed`/`error.signal` as plausible places to check, but rather than assume either was the right one, this was tested directly against this project's actual Node version before writing any detection code: `execFileSync('sleep', ['5'], { timeout: 1000 })` was run and its caught error inspected field-by-field. The result: `error.killed` was `undefined` — **not** `true` as Node's own higher-level docs might suggest for a general "process was killed" case — while `error.code` was reliably `"ETIMEDOUT"` and `error.signal` was `"SIGTERM"`. Relying on `killed` would have meant every real timeout in this Node version silently fell through to the generic `"error"` bucket, defeating the entire point of this phase. `signal === "SIGTERM"` alone was also tested and rejected as the primary check: a companion test confirmed a plain nonzero-exit failure (no timeout at all) reports `signal: null`, so `SIGTERM` specifically *would* have worked as a marker in this instance, but `code === "ETIMEDOUT"` is the more precise, Node-documented signal for exactly this condition (a subprocess killed because `execFileSync`'s own `timeout` option elapsed) rather than an artifact of which signal Node happens to default to for it — code is what to check if the kill signal ever changed (e.g. via `killSignal`).
./DECISIONS.md:397:**Alternatives considered:** `error.killed === true` — rejected outright per the empirical test above; it's `undefined`, not `true`, for this exact scenario in this Node version. `error.signal === "SIGTERM"` as the primary/sole check — considered, since it does empirically distinguish a timeout from a plain nonzero exit in the tests run here, but `code` is the more semantically precise signal (tied to *why* the process was killed, not *how*) and doesn't depend on SIGTERM specifically remaining the default kill signal. A combined check (`code === "ETIMEDOUT" && signal === "SIGTERM"`) — redundant given `code` alone was sufficient and correctly discriminating in every test case run; adding the second condition would only create a way for a genuine timeout to be missed if some environment ever surfaces `ETIMEDOUT` with a different signal value.
./DECISIONS.md:403:**Finding:** Hook subprocesses (`PreToolUse`/`PostToolUse`/`Stop` — everything Grasp has run inside through Phase 6) have **no controlling terminal at all**. This is stated directly and unambiguously in Claude Code's official docs (`code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks`, fetched live): *"On macOS and Linux, command hooks run in their own session without a controlling terminal as of v2.1.139. The hook process and any child processes can't open `/dev/tty` or send escape sequences directly to the Claude Code interface."* The docs state directly that *"hooks cannot prompt interactively."* stdin is the JSON payload (already known from Phase 3); stdout/stderr are captured text relayed into Claude Code's own transcript, not a live terminal a user is watching in real time. The only channel for a hook to touch the terminal at all is a narrowly-restricted `terminalSequence` field limited to OSC window-title/bell/notification sequences — explicitly *not* cursor movement, colors, or anything an interactive UI would need. Blocking (`PreToolUse` exit code 2, or JSON `permissionDecision: "deny"`) is confirmed to be a **pass/fail signal Claude Code reads after the hook exits**, not a live session the user interacts with while the hook is running — Claude Code pauses, waits for the hook process to *exit*, and reads its exit code/stdout as the verdict.
./DECISIONS.md:405:**How this was established:** Fetched the official docs live and independently cross-checked the finding with a separate web search pulling from the same docs page plus a third-party detailed hooks guide — both corroborate the "without a controlling terminal" / "can't open /dev/tty" / "cannot prompt interactively" language verbatim. Also re-confirmed (per this task's explicit instruction to re-check, not assume it still holds) that no authenticated live Claude Code CLI is available in this sandboxed environment (`claude auth status` still reports `"loggedIn": false"`, unchanged since Phase 3) — so this finding rests on two independently-corroborated, freshly-fetched documentation sources rather than a live-fire test, which is the same evidentiary standard already used for the hook payload shape in Phase 3.
./DECISIONS.md:407:**Why this breaks Phase 7 as scoped:** BUILD_PLAN.md's Phase 7 describes rendering an `ink` TUI that captures raw keypresses and blocks the hook's own exit until the user answers or explicitly skips, most likely from `Stop` (already wired to `onSessionComplete`). An `ink` app needs a real controlling terminal to put into raw mode and render to — `Stop` (or any hook) has neither: there's no `/dev/tty` to attach to, and stdin is the JSON hook payload, not a live keyboard stream. Building the TUI to run *inside* the hook process the way Phase 7 describes would render nothing the user could see and read nothing the user could type — it wouldn't fail loudly, it would just silently not work, which is worse than not building it at all.
./DECISIONS.md:409:**Decision:** Do not build Phase 7 as originally scoped. Two concrete alternative architectures are proposed to the user rather than picked unilaterally (this is a product-shape decision, not an implementation detail) — see the session report for the options. No TUI code, no `ink` dependency, and no other Phase 7 implementation was written this session; this entry and the report back are the full output of this phase's work so far.
./DECISIONS.md:411:**Alternatives considered (for the finding itself, not the architecture):** Trust the brief's/BUILD_PLAN's original assumption that hooks have terminal access, since it was never explicitly verified in Phases 1-6 — rejected, since the task explicitly requires resolving this before writing TUI code, and every one of Phases 2/3/5/6's own "verify empirically, don't assume" precedents point the same direction. Attempt a live empirical test anyway despite the known auth constraint (e.g. trying to force a `/login` flow) — rejected for the same reasons already logged in Phase 3's payload-shape entry: out of bounds, not what was asked, and risks using the user's account for a side purpose without being asked; in this case also unnecessary, since the documentation is unusually explicit and internally consistent on this specific point (it doesn't just fail to mention TTY access — it explains the exact failure mode of trying to use it, `/dev/tty` writes failing, and provides a narrow escape-sequence-only workaround specifically because direct terminal access isn't available).
./DECISIONS.md:415:## 2026-08-05 — Phase 7 builds `grasp review` only, not `grasp watch`
./DECISIONS.md:417:**Decision:** This phase implements the on-demand `grasp review` command (user runs it when they choose to work through pending questions). It does not implement `grasp watch` (a persistent process that auto-pops the TUI the moment a new question lands) — that variant was proposed as one flavor of the corrected architecture but is explicitly out of scope for this session, per direct instruction.
./DECISIONS.md:419:**Reasoning:** `review` alone is sufficient to prove out the corrected architecture end-to-end — the interactive TUI, free-text answer capture, skip friction, and hard-gate enforcement all work identically regardless of whether presentation is on-demand or auto-popping; `watch` would mostly add a polling/file-watching loop and process-lifecycle management around the same rendering code, not new core mechanics. Building only the on-demand variant first, then deciding whether `watch` is worth adding once `review` has seen real use, matches the project's own standing bias (see the brief's §4 deferred-items framing throughout) toward earning expansion with usage signal rather than building ahead of it.
./DECISIONS.md:421:**Note for whoever picks up `watch` (or Phase 8) later:** Phase 8's existing text ("the accumulated queue... is presented via the Phase 7 TUI... before the agent's final output is shown") was written under the original in-hook-TUI assumption and describes synchronous, blocking presentation. Under this corrected architecture, presentation happens in a separate, user-launched `review` process — there's no way for anything to be shown "before the agent's final output" in the literal blocking sense the original phrasing implies, only via the `Stop` hook's `systemMessage` nudge (already built this phase) plus whatever `review`/`watch` does next. This wasn't fixed here — the task scoped this session to Phase 7 and explicitly said not to touch Phase 8 — but it's flagged the same way Phase 3 flagged its own consequence for later phases, so it isn't silently inherited as still-accurate.
./DECISIONS.md:423:**Alternatives considered:** Build both `review` and `watch` together, since they're closely related — rejected per explicit instruction and because `review` alone is enough to validate the architecture; building `watch` without real usage of `review` first risks guessing at a UX (auto-pop timing, how intrusive it should be) instead of learning it.
./DECISIONS.md:429:**Decision:** `ink@^5.2.1` and `react@^18.3.1` (plus `ink-text-input@^6.0.0` for free-text input) are used for `grasp review`'s TUI. Since these are ESM-only (`"type": "module"`) while the rest of the project stays CommonJS (per the Phase 1 "TypeScript compiler target and module system" decision, unchanged), they're loaded at runtime via `await eval('import("ink")')` — a narrow interop shim confined to the one module that needs it (`src/inkLoader.ts`) — rather than converting the whole project to ESM.
./DECISIONS.md:439:**Decision:** `src/ink-ambient.d.ts` hand-declares a narrow `declare module "ink" { ... }` / `declare module "ink-text-input" { ... }` covering only the API surface `grasp review` actually uses (`Box`, `Text`, `render`, `useInput`, `useApp`, the default `TextInput` export, and their prop/option shapes) — rather than importing the packages' real, more complete `.d.ts` files.
./DECISIONS.md:443:**Alternatives considered:** Switch `moduleResolution` to `"bundler"` (and `module` to `"esnext"`/`"preserve"`) project-wide — rejected as a second, larger reopening of the Phase 1 module-system decision in the same session as the `eval`-import workaround above; two separate ESM-interop workarounds in one phase for one new dependency is worse than one narrow ambient-types file. Reference the packages' real `.d.ts` files by a relative subpath import (e.g. `"ink/build/index"`) — tried and also failed to resolve under classic resolution; not worth pursuing further once the ambient-declarations approach was confirmed to work cleanly. Use `// @ts-ignore`/`any` at each ink usage site instead of real types — rejected as strictly worse: no type safety at all for the one new UI surface in the codebase, versus a few dozen lines of narrow, accurate-where-it-matters ambient types.
./DECISIONS.md:447:## 2026-08-05 — `grasp review` query scope: global across all repos, not scoped to the current working directory
./DECISIONS.md:449:**Decision:** `getPendingQuestions` (the query backing `grasp review`) returns unanswered/unskipped real questions across **every repo**, not filtered to whatever repo the command happens to be run from.
./DECISIONS.md:451:**Reasoning:** Two things point the same direction. First, precedent: concept-tag memoization is already explicitly global ("a concept answered on one project counts as taught everywhere," Phase 1/5) — Grasp's mastery model is about the *user*, not a per-project ledger, and scoping `review` to one repo while memoization stays global would be an inconsistent mental model for the same underlying data. Second, and more concretely: `review` doesn't technically *need* to run from within a matching repo directory at all, because everything it renders — diff summary, hunks, question text — is already self-contained in the stored `events`/`diffFiles` data (this phase's own design goal: "reuse the already-stored data, don't re-fetch"). Since correctness doesn't depend on cwd, an artificial repo filter would only add a way to *lose track* of pending questions in a repo the user isn't currently sitting in — exactly the failure mode a global, inbox-style view avoids. Each rendered question clearly labels its own `repo`, so there's no ambiguity about context even when reviewing something from a project the user isn't currently in.
./DECISIONS.md:457:## 2026-08-05 — `PreToolUse`/`Stop` gate-check scope: `session_id`, not global or per-turn
./DECISIONS.md:459:**Decision:** Both the `PreToolUse` hard-gate check and the `Stop` `systemMessage` nudge query `getPendingQuestionsForSession` — scoped to the current Claude Code `session_id` — not global across all sessions/repos, and not narrowed to just the current turn (`prompt_id`).
./DECISIONS.md:461:**Reasoning:** `session_id`-scoping (not global) matches the cost cap's own already-established boundary (Phase 5) and, independently, is the only scope that doesn't risk the exact "punishment tool" failure mode brief §3.1 warns about: if hard-gate checked *globally*, an unanswered question left over from a repo touched days ago could block work in a completely unrelated project today — a user would experience that as arbitrary and unfair, not as "teach, don't punish." Scoping to *this* session's own outstanding work is what "next command blocked until answered" (brief §3.1) actually describes — you shouldn't be able to keep telling Claude Code to do more work in the same sitting that produced an unanswered question, but an old question from a different sitting shouldn't reach into today's work at all. Not narrowing further to just the current turn (`prompt_id`) matters too: `Stop` fires once per turn, so if turn 1 generates a question and the user hasn't answered it by turn 2's `PreToolUse`, a turn-scoped check would let them simply move on and never be asked again within that sitting — checking the whole `session_id` (every turn so far) is what makes hard-gate actually mean "must engage before proceeding," not "can dodge by starting a new turn."
./DECISIONS.md:463:**Alternatives considered:** Global scope (any pending question anywhere blocks any tool call anywhere) — rejected per the cross-project-punishment reasoning above. Per-turn (`(session_id, prompt_id)`) scope — rejected because it would let a user escape an unanswered question from an earlier turn simply by continuing to a new one in the same session, undermining hard-gate's actual purpose. Repo-scoped (any pending question for this repo, regardless of session) — considered, since it's a plausible middle ground, but rejected as solving a problem `session_id`-scoping already solves more precisely: two different sessions in the same repo on different days are exactly the kind of "different sitting" hard-gate shouldn't reach across, the same reasoning that ruled out global scope.
./DECISIONS.md:469:**Decision:** `grasp review`'s diff view colors lines by their *diff role* — green for additions, red for deletions, cyan for hunk headers, default/dim for context and file headers — not by language-aware syntax tokenization (keywords, strings, types, etc. individually colored per the file's actual language).
./DECISIONS.md:471:**Reasoning:** Brief §3.4 asks for "syntax-highlighted diffs" without further specification. Diff-role coloring is what the overwhelming majority of real terminal diff tools actually mean by a "colored"/"highlighted" diff (`git diff`'s own default output, `delta`, `diff-so-fancy`, GitHub's diff view before its own optional per-language layer) — it's the legible, load-bearing signal (what was added vs. removed vs. unchanged context) that directly serves the actual goal here: helping the user read and understand a change fast. True per-language tokenization would need either a dependency capable of lexing arbitrary, often-mixed languages across a diff's files (Grasp has no way to know a file's language beyond its extension, and hunks frequently span multiple languages across one multi-file diff) or a much larger hand-rolled effort, for a benefit (keyword/string/type coloring on top of already-clear add/remove coloring) that's real but secondary to the diff-role signal. Given `ink` is already a first-time, non-trivial new dependency this phase, adding a second heavier one for a nice-to-have layer the brief doesn't explicitly require felt like the wrong place to spend that budget.
./DECISIONS.md:479:**Decision:** In `grasp review`, pressing the skip key (Escape) at any point while working through a `question_type: "both"` event — whether during the concept question or the instance question, even after already typing (but not yet submitting) an answer to the other one — marks the *entire event* skipped. There's no partial state where, say, the concept question is recorded as answered but the instance question is recorded as skipped (or vice versa).
./DECISIONS.md:481:**Reasoning:** This follows directly from the existing schema, not from a new design choice made from scratch: `events` has exactly one `skipped` boolean and one `skip_reason` per row (Phase 1's schema, brief §3.5's own field list), with no equivalent per-sub-question granularity. Redesigning the schema to support a partial-skip state would be a real, non-trivial schema change this phase wasn't asked to make (and BUILD_PLAN.md's Phase 7 scope doesn't call for it either) — so the only choice actually available within the existing schema was how to collapse a partial-skip *interaction* onto the existing binary skipped/answered state, and "the whole event is skipped, whatever was typed for the other sub-question is discarded" is the simplest, least-surprising collapse: it matches what "skip" means everywhere else in the product (an explicit, deliberate act that says "not now," not a selective per-field save).
./DECISIONS.md:483:**Alternatives considered:** Silently persist whatever partial answer text existed at skip time into `answer_concept`/`answer_instance` anyway, while still setting `skipped = 1` — rejected as confusing: a row that's simultaneously `skipped = 1` and has real answer text would misrepresent what actually happened (the user didn't finish answering, they skipped) and would complicate any future logic that treats `skipped` as authoritative for "was this genuinely engaged with." Redesign the schema now to track concept/instance answer-vs-skip independently — rejected as real, unrequested scope growth in a phase whose task explicitly enumerates what to build; worth reconsidering only if real usage of `review` shows partial engagement (answer one, skip the other) is common enough to matter.
./DECISIONS.md:487:## 2026-08-05 — Phase 8 reinterpreted under the `review`-based architecture
./DECISIONS.md:489:**Decision:** BUILD_PLAN.md's Phase 8 section is rewritten to drop its original "accumulate a per-turn queue, present it synchronously before the agent's final output" mechanism. Under the corrected architecture (Phase 7's TTY finding — hooks can't render anything), there is no synchronous moment for a queue to be flushed into; presentation only ever happens when the user runs `grasp review`. "Batching" (brief §5.3) is redefined as two narrower things this phase actually builds: (1) a session-wide questions-per-session cap that keeps a long run's backlog bounded, and (2) `grasp review` presenting a real accumulated batch in a coherently grouped/ordered way. The section's underlying goal — content should scale with a long run's amount of change, but not pile up unanswerably — is kept explicitly, only the mechanism description changes.
./DECISIONS.md:491:**Reasoning:** This is the direct, previously-flagged follow-up to Phase 7's own scoping note ("Phase 8's existing text... was written under the original in-hook-TUI assumption and describes synchronous, blocking presentation... this wasn't fixed here... but it's flagged the same way Phase 3 flagged its own consequence for later phases, so it isn't silently inherited as still-accurate"). Leaving the old text in place while building something structurally different under the same heading would be exactly the kind of silently-stale doc Phase 3's own flagging precedent exists to prevent — BUILD_PLAN.md is supposed to describe what's actually being built, not what an earlier, now-invalidated architecture assumed. The "scale content, cap the backlog" *goal* itself was never actually invalidated by the TTY finding — only the specific "synchronous reveal" delivery mechanism was — so keeping the goal language intact while replacing the mechanism description is a correction, not a scope change.
./DECISIONS.md:493:**Alternatives considered:** Leave Phase 8's text as originally written and just build something different under it — rejected per the explicit task instruction and the same reasoning that motivated Phase 7's own doc rewrite: an unbuildable synchronous-queue description left in place would actively mislead whoever reads it next. Try to actually build some form of synchronous pre-output presentation (e.g. having the `Stop` hook block until `grasp review` is run and exits) — rejected as reopening the Phase 7 architecture decision itself, which is explicitly not this session's task, and doesn't fit "hooks can't prompt interactively" regardless of how the blocking is arranged.
./DECISIONS.md:497:## 2026-08-05 — Questions-per-session cap miss-reason: reuse `cap_reached`, not a new value
./DECISIONS.md:499:**Decision:** When `runGeneration` (`src/generation.ts`) skips generation because the session-wide question count already meets `questionsPerSessionCap`, the resulting miss row uses `miss_reason: "cap_reached"` — the same value the cost cap already uses — rather than a new, more specific value (e.g. `"question_cap_reached"`).
./DECISIONS.md:501:**Reasoning:** Brief §3.5 defines exactly three `miss_reason` values (`error`/`timeout`/`cap_reached`), and the task for this phase explicitly warned against inventing a fourth without real justification, since that's schema drift the brief didn't ask for. `cap_reached` was originally written for the cost cap alone, but its plain-English meaning — "a configured limit was hit before generation could run" — describes the question-count cap just as accurately; nothing about the value's name is cost-specific. Distinguishing *which* cap actually fired for a given `cap_reached` row doesn't need a new column or a new enum value at all: it's already fully recoverable after the fact by comparing that session's cumulative `SUM(cost_usd)` against `costCapUsd` and its `question_type IS NOT NULL` count against `questionsPerSessionCap`, both as of that row's `timestamp` (in practice, checking the session's *current* totals is sufficient too, since a cap that was hit stays hit — sessions don't un-spend money or un-ask questions). Both queries are one-liners against data already being recorded for other reasons (`events.cost_usd`, `events.session_id`, `events.question_type`), so the distinction costs nothing extra to reconstruct, and manual verification step 2 for this phase confirms exactly that.
./DECISIONS.md:503:**Alternatives considered:** A new `miss_reason: "question_cap_reached"`, keeping `cap_reached` cost-specific — the most literal reading of "keep them distinguishable," and arguably clearer at a glance without needing to cross-reference other columns. Rejected because it's the kind of schema growth the task explicitly said needed a real justification, and "slightly more convenient to eyeball" isn't one when the same information is one cheap query away — brief §3.5's three-value vocabulary is a deliberately small, closed set, and widening it for a distinction the data already encodes elsewhere trades schema stability for a minor DX convenience. A `miss_reason: "cap_reached"` plus a new nullable `cap_kind` column (`"cost" | "questions"`) — a middle ground that keeps the brief's three values intact while making the distinction a direct column read instead of a derived comparison; rejected as still unrequested schema growth for a distinction that's already reliably derivable, and because it would need its own migration for existing databases for marginal query convenience.
./DECISIONS.md:507:## 2026-08-05 — `grasp review` batch grouping/ordering: by session, not a flat timestamp list
./DECISIONS.md:509:**Decision:** `grasp review`'s pending-question list (`groupForBatchPresentation`, `src/review.ts`) is grouped by `session_id` before rendering: all of one session's pending questions stay adjacent, session-groups themselves are ordered by when each group's earliest question was generated, and questions within a group stay oldest-first (inherited from `getPendingQuestions`' existing timestamp-ASC order — no separate re-sort needed there). Events with a null `session_id` (currently only possible via `debug:seed`) each become their own singleton group rather than being merged with each other or interleaved into a real session's group. The rendered header shows the overall position ("N of M pending") plus, whenever there's more than one question in the current session's group or more than one session in the batch, a session-context line ("session X of Y — question A of B for this session").
./DECISIONS.md:511:**Reasoning:** The task's own framing is "make a real accumulated batch read coherently, not like an arbitrary list" — a flat global timestamp order (what Phase 7 shipped, and what `getPendingQuestions` alone still returns) actively works against that whenever more than one session contributes pending questions: two sessions' questions would end up interleaved by whenever each happened to be generated, forcing the user to mentally context-switch between unrelated diffs every other question instead of working through one coherent unit of related work before moving to the next. Grouping by `session_id` is the natural unit because it's also the boundary every other cap/scope decision in this project already uses (cost cap, question cap, hard-gate check) — reusing it here rather than inventing a different grouping key (e.g. by repo, or by day) keeps "session" as the one consistent notion of "a coherent unit of related work" throughout the whole codebase. Ordering *groups* by earliest-question-in-group (rather than, say, alphabetically by session id, or by group size) preserves the same "oldest first" intuition the flat list already had, just applied one level up — a user working through a real backlog naturally expects to clear older work first. This is presentation logic, not a query concern, so it lives in `review.ts` (which already owns TUI wiring) operating on `getPendingQuestions`' existing global, timestamp-ordered result, rather than pushing a `GROUP BY`/window-function query into `store.ts` — the store's job is still just "give me every pending question," and how that gets organized for one specific presentation surface is `review`'s own concern, matching Phase 7's existing store/UI split.
./DECISIONS.md:513:**Alternatives considered:** A SQL-level `ORDER BY session_id, timestamp` in `getPendingQuestions` itself — simpler (no client-side grouping code), but rejected on two counts: it sorts session groups lexicographically by session-id string rather than by which session's work is actually oldest, which is the wrong "oldest first" for groups even though it's right within a group; and it would hardcode one specific presentation's ordering preference into a store function whose only other caller (the `PreToolUse`/`Stop` hooks' `getPendingQuestionsForSession`, technically a different function, but the same table) has no use for it. Group by repo instead of session — considered since `review`'s own header already prints `event.repo` per question, but rejected because a single session can span work the user cares about together even if it happened to touch more than one repo in one sitting, whereas two unrelated sessions in the *same* repo, days apart, are exactly the kind of thing that shouldn't be forced into one coherent-feeling batch (this mirrors the reasoning in the existing "gate-check scope" entry for why hard-gate uses `session_id`, not repo). No grouping at all, just a richer per-line label (e.g. prefixing each question with a short session id) so the user can visually track session boundaries without actually reordering — rejected as a weaker version of the same idea: it still leaves genuinely related questions scattered through the list by raw timestamp, which is the actual coherence problem being solved here, not just a labeling gap.
./DECISIONS.md:519:**Decision:** Brief §3.3's "cumulative session spend visible to the user, not just logged" is implemented by extending the existing `Stop` handler (`src/cli.ts`) to also compute `getSessionCostUsd(db, sessionId)` and, whenever it's greater than 0, append a cost-summary sentence (`costSummaryMessage`) to the `systemMessage` — in the same message as the Phase 7 pending-question nudge when both apply, as one combined sentence, not two separate `systemMessage`s or a second hook field. Each half is independently gated: pending-count text appears only if there's a real pending question; cost text appears only if the session has actually spent anything (`> 0`, not merely "cap not yet hit" — a session that captured diffs but never had a passing one, or whose diffs were all filtered out, spent nothing and says nothing). If neither applies, `Stop` emits no `systemMessage` at all, same as before this phase. Cost is formatted to 4 decimal places (`$0.0043 spent...`), not the conventional 2.
./DECISIONS.md:521:**Reasoning:** Given Phase 7's TTY finding (hooks can't render anything interactively), `Stop`'s `systemMessage` is — as already established for the pending-question nudge — the only channel a hook has to say anything to the user at all, so it's the only real candidate for "visible to the user" in brief §3.3's sense; there is no other surface that fires automatically without the user having to remember to run something. Combining into the existing message (rather than adding a second hook output field, which doesn't exist, or firing the nudge and the cost line as separate `Stop` invocations, which isn't possible — `Stop` fires once) is simplest and avoids inventing new hook-output shape for no reason. Gating the cost line on `> 0` rather than always showing it (even as "$0.00 spent") matters because `Stop` fires on *every* turn, including turns where nothing meaningful was captured at all (a pure `Read`/`Grep` turn, or one where every diff was filtered) — showing "$0.00 spent" on every single quiet turn would be exactly the kind of low-signal noise that trains users to stop reading `systemMessage`s altogether, undermining the one channel Grasp has. 4-decimal formatting (rather than the usual 2) is a direct consequence of the numbers involved: `runGeneration`'s own real judge+generate calls cost a fraction of a cent each (see the mock-`claude` verification data from Phase 5/8's own testing, routinely `$0.001`–`$0.003`), so 2-decimal formatting would round the overwhelming majority of real per-session totals to `$0.00` even when real spend happened — defeating the entire point of a spend-transparency line by hiding exactly the number it exists to show.
./DECISIONS.md:523:**Alternatives considered:** A separate, always-present `systemMessage` on every `Stop` regardless of amount (e.g. "$0.00 spent so far") — rejected per the noise reasoning above; a cap/spend-transparency signal that fires constantly regardless of whether there's anything to say trains users to ignore it, which is worse for the brief's actual "spend transparency" goal than only speaking up when there's something to report. A dedicated `grasp cost` (or similar) command the user runs on demand, mirroring `grasp review` — considered, since it would also let a user check spend outside of an active Claude Code turn, but rejected as solving a problem the brief doesn't actually raise (brief §3.3 asks for visibility, not an on-demand ledger view) and as duplicating data `sqlite3 ~/.grasp/history.db` can already answer directly per §3.5's inspectability stance — worth adding later only if a real user asks to check spend outside of `Stop`'s cadence. 2-decimal formatting for conventional-currency-looking output — rejected per the "rounds real spend to zero" reasoning above; correctness of the actual number shown matters more here than matching typical `$X.XX` currency convention.
./DECISIONS.md:531:**Reasoning:** `grasp init` is the one command in the entire codebase that both (a) has genuine interactive terminal access (it's a real, user-launched CLI invocation, same category as `grasp review` — not a hook subprocess) and (b) is a hard prerequisite for generation ever running at all: `runGeneration` only ever executes from inside `ClaudeCodeAdapter.onChangeDetected`, which only ever runs from a `PostToolUse` hook firing, which can only fire if Claude Code has the hook registered — and the only code path that registers it is `grasp init` writing `.claude/settings.local.json`. There is no sequence of events in which a real generation call happens without `grasp init` having run and been confirmed first, so "before any question could possibly be generated" (this phase's own verification-step wording) is not just plausible but structurally guaranteed by where the message already sits, not merely likely. Every other candidate location fails one of the two properties: `ensureInitialized()` (runs on literally every `grasp` invocation, including `--version`/`--help`) has no interactive gate and would print the same disclosure on commands that have nothing to do with generation, training users to skim past it before they ever reach the one moment it actually matters; a hook itself (`PreToolUse`/`Stop`) has no controlling terminal (Phase 7's finding) and fires *after* the moment consent should have happened, not before.
./DECISIONS.md:533:**Alternatives considered:** Also print the message on every `grasp review`/first pending-question view — rejected as redundant and late: by the time a real question exists to review, generation has already happened at least once, so repeating the disclosure there is a receipt, not a consent gate: the brief's own framing ("first-run… before…") is about informing before the behavior starts, not after. Print it once on the very first invocation of *any* `grasp` command (tracked via a marker file) — rejected because it decouples the disclosure from the actual moment of consequence (hook installation); a user could see and dismiss it while merely running `grasp --version` out of curiosity, long before deciding whether to run `init` at all, which weakens rather than strengthens the "state plainly, don't bury it" requirement. Leave it undocumented outside `init` entirely, relying on the README alone — rejected outright; brief §3.3 explicitly asks for both a first-run *message* and docs, not one or the other.
./DECISIONS.md:539:**Decision:** Testing `ignorePatterns` end-to-end (not just reading `filter.ts` and assuming it was wired) surfaced a real bug: a repo's own `.grasp.json` — an untracked file sitting in the working tree the moment a user creates one — gets swept into `captureGitDiff`'s union of untracked files (Phase 2's own, still-correct decision) like any other file. Nothing in `BASELINE_IGNORE_PATTERNS` (`src/ignoreBaseline.ts`) excluded it, so in a real test run where `.grasp.json` was the only untracked/changed file with real content, it became the diff's sole "significant file," passed the mechanical filter cleanly, and reached the judge call — which (correctly, given what it was handed) produced a real question about Grasp's own config file rather than anything the AI agent actually did. Fixed by adding `.grasp.json` to `BASELINE_IGNORE_PATTERNS`, matched by exact basename the same way `package-lock.json` and friends already are — confirmed by re-running the exact same scenario post-fix and observing the diff summary correctly contain only the real file that changed.
./DECISIONS.md:543:**Alternatives considered:** Exclude `.grasp.json` at the git-diff-capture layer instead of the filter layer (i.e., never let `captureGitDiff` pick it up as an untracked file in the first place) — rejected because Phase 2's untracked-file union is deliberately adapter-agnostic and content-blind (it doesn't know what "Grasp's own config" means, only "files git sees"); teaching the filter layer, which already owns exactly this kind of "files that don't count" judgment for lockfiles/generated paths, keeps the special-casing in one place instead of two. Have `grasp init`/first config-write add `.grasp.json` to the *user's own* `ignorePatterns` array automatically — rejected as solving a universal problem (every install has this file) with a per-repo, user-editable mechanism a user could accidentally remove, when a baseline entry is unconditional and requires no such bookkeeping. Leave it as user-facing guidance ("remember to gitignore your `.grasp.json`") instead of a code fix — considered, since `captureGitDiff`'s untracked-file listing does use `git ls-files --others --exclude-standard` (confirmed by testing: adding `.grasp.json` to a scratch repo's `.gitignore` does stop it appearing in the untracked-file list), so gitignoring it per-repo would in fact have worked. Rejected anyway because it pushes a universal problem (every install has this file, every time) onto per-user diligence a new user has no way to know they need — the same reasoning that already put lockfiles/`node_modules/` in the baseline list rather than expecting every user's own `.gitignore` to cover them; a baseline fix protects every install unconditionally, a documentation-only fix protects only the installs that happened to read and follow it.
./DECISIONS.md:547:## 2026-08-05 — Codex fix pass: checkpoint-based incremental capture (git tree-vs-tree diffing)
./DECISIONS.md:549:**Decision:** `ClaudeCodeAdapter.checkAndCapture()` no longer diffs the working tree against `HEAD` on every `PostToolUse` firing. It now diffs against a per-`(session_id, repo)` checkpoint stored in a new `capture_checkpoints` table (`src/store.ts`), advancing that checkpoint to the current state after every capture. The checkpoint itself is a real git **tree object** built via a throwaway scratch index (`GIT_INDEX_FILE` pointed at a temp file, never the repo's real index) — `writeWorktreeTree()` in `src/adapters/gitDiffCapture.ts` runs `read-tree <base>`, `add -A`, `write-tree` against that scratch index and returns the resulting tree SHA, capturing tracked *and* untracked (non-gitignored) content in one object with no separate untracked-file-union step needed afterward. Two such snapshots are diffed against each other (`captureDiffBetweenTrees`, tree-ish vs tree-ish) using the exact same `git diff --name-status`/`--numstat`/raw invocations `captureGitDiff` already used for `HEAD`-vs-worktree, since git's diff output format is identical either way. The checkpoint baseline for a session+repo is seeded on the *first* hook firing Grasp ever observes for it (any event type — `ClaudeCodeAdapter.ensureCheckpointSeeded()`, called unconditionally at the top of every hook invocation in `cli.ts`, the same place `ensureTurnStarted()` already runs), specifically *before* `PostToolUse`/`checkAndCapture` can ever run — so the very first tool call's own changes are never silently swallowed into "already seen." `events.diff_hash` is now populated as `${fromTreeSha}..${toTreeSha}` — git's own range-diff notation, a real, reproducible identifier — instead of always `NULL`.
./DECISIONS.md:551:**Reasoning:** This directly fixes Codex's #1 critical finding: every `PostToolUse` re-diffing the whole tree against `HEAD` meant two firings with no new work between them produced the same captured diff, the same generated question, and the same charge twice, and could attribute pre-existing uncommitted work (present before the agent's session even started) to the agent. The brief itself (§5.1) describes the fallback capture path as "watch git working-tree state / diff between checkpoints," and Phase 2's own "Git-diff capture: comparison mechanism" entry explicitly flagged this as deferred rather than solved ("there's no poll loop or persistent capture-daemon yet for 'checkpoints' to mean anything... a real checkpoint concept is more naturally Phase 3's problem once hooks define actual event boundaries to checkpoint against") — Phase 3 then built the hook plumbing but never actually closed that loop, which is exactly the gap this fixes now that a real trigger (`PostToolUse`) and a real state-persistence layer (SQLite, already used for `cc_turns`/`captured_diffs`) both exist. The git-tree-object mechanism specifically (rather than a raw file-content snapshot or a plain hash comparison) was chosen after direct empirical testing, not assumption: a naive alternative — `git diff <tree>` against the live worktree — was tried first and found to **misreport untracked files as deleted**, because that form of `git diff` is actually a tree-vs-*index* comparison under the hood (augmented by worktree stat info for paths already in the index), and the real index never has untracked entries regardless of what the checkpoint tree contains. Diffing two independently-built tree snapshots against each other sidesteps the index entirely and was confirmed correct for modifications, additions, deletions, and untracked-file transitions before being wired into the adapter. Building the tree via a scratch `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (not the real index) matters because it's the standard, safe technique for snapshotting a worktree without ever making Grasp's own bookkeeping visible to `git status`/`git commit` — the repo's real index is never touched. Seeding the checkpoint on the *first hook firing of any type* (not lazily inside `checkAndCapture`) matters because `checkAndCapture` only runs on `PostToolUse`, i.e. *after* a tool call already ran; seeding there would bake that first tool call's own work into the "nothing new yet" baseline and silently lose it. In ordinary Claude Code operation `PreToolUse` fires before the first tool call, so seeding unconditionally at the top of every hook (same place `ensureTurnStarted` already runs, itself idempotent) reliably captures the true pre-agent-work state.
./DECISIONS.md:553:**Alternatives considered:** A raw file-content snapshot (e.g. a JSON map of path → content or hash, stored per session+repo) compared by Grasp's own diffing logic — rejected as reinventing git's own diff engine (rename detection, hunk splitting, binary handling) for no benefit over asking git directly, the same reasoning Phase 2 already used to justify shelling out to git rather than hand-rolling diff logic. A simple hash-of-the-cumulative-diff comparison (dedupe only when the *whole* diff-vs-HEAD is byte-identical to last time) — rejected because it only catches the exact "nothing changed at all" case Codex demonstrated; it does nothing for the equally-real "a later small change gets mixed together with every earlier uncommitted change" complaint, since the comparison base (`HEAD`) never moves, so every capture would still include all prior changes, just occasionally deduped as a whole rather than truly incremental. Keying the checkpoint per `(session_id, prompt_id)` (per turn) instead of per `(session_id, repo)` — rejected because a turn boundary isn't a meaningful "already seen" boundary: several tool calls within one turn should each surface only their own new work, and resetting the checkpoint at every turn boundary would reintroduce partial duplication (the first `PostToolUse` of turn 2 would re-report turn 1's tail-end changes if they hadn't been checkpointed yet). Not touching `captureGitDiff`/`GitDiffAdapter` at all — kept as-is deliberately: it backs `debug:capture`, a manual, one-shot "show me everything uncommitted right now" inspection command with no session concept to checkpoint against, and changing its semantics would break that command's actual purpose.
./DECISIONS.md:557:## 2026-08-05 — Codex fix pass: blank-answer submissions rejected, not silently recorded as engagement
./DECISIONS.md:559:**Decision:** In `grasp review` (`src/reviewApp.tsx`), submitting the concept or instance answer prompt with blank/whitespace-only text is now a no-op — the app stays in answering mode, shows a yellow hint ("A blank answer isn't accepted — type something, or press Esc to skip instead."), and does **not** advance state, mark the event answered, or flip any `concept_tags.answered` value. The skip-reason prompt is explicitly exempted from this check and still accepts a blank submission (pressing Enter with no text there still completes a skip with `skip_reason: NULL`) — the reason text is optional per brief §3.1; only the answer text itself must be real.
./DECISIONS.md:561:**Reasoning:** Codex reproduced a real bypass: pressing Enter with nothing typed for both the concept and instance prompts wrote empty strings into `answer_concept`/`answer_instance`, set `skipped = 0`, and flipped the linked `concept_tags.answered` to `1` — permanently marking a concept "learned" globally, across every future repo, with zero actual engagement, and via a path (blank Enter) that's easier to hit than the deliberate skip flow the whole product design (brief §3.1, "never make skipping free") is built around. Blocking submission (rather than routing a blank submission through the skip flow automatically) was chosen because silently reinterpreting "Enter with nothing typed" as an implicit skip is its own kind of surprising behavior — a user who fat-fingers Enter expects nothing to happen, not to have quietly skipped and been asked "why are you skipping?" for an action they didn't consciously take. Simply not submitting, with a visible reason why, keeps the two real actions (answer for real, or press Escape to skip on purpose) exactly as distinct and deliberate as the brief already requires skipping to be.
./DECISIONS.md:563:**Alternatives considered:** Route a blank submission through the existing skip flow instead of blocking it — the task's own suggested alternative; rejected per the "implicit skip via a typo is surprising" reasoning above, and because it would need its own state transition (jumping from "answering the concept question" straight to "skip-reason, already-blank") that reads as a stranger UX than just not submitting. Trim-and-accept (store the trimmed value even if it was originally whitespace-only, i.e. as an empty string) — rejected as identical to the original bug in effect, since an empty string is exactly what was being wrongly accepted before. Show a full validation-error style message permanently (not just while the input is blank) — rejected as unnecessary; the hint only needs to appear when it's actually relevant (current input is blank), which is what the code does by conditioning the hint on `inputValue.trim().length === 0`.
./DECISIONS.md:567:## 2026-08-05 — Codex fix pass: `.claude/settings.local.json` baseline-ignored, same class of bug as `.grasp.json`
./DECISIONS.md:569:**Decision:** Added `.claude/settings.local.json` (matched by its exact relative path, the same way `matchesIgnorePattern` already matches `.grasp.json`) to `BASELINE_IGNORE_PATTERNS`.
./DECISIONS.md:571:**Reasoning:** Identical bug shape to Phase 9's `.grasp.json` fix, just for the *other* file Grasp itself writes: `grasp init` creates `.claude/settings.local.json`, and if it's left uncommitted/untracked (the norm — it's meant to stay local per the Phase 3 "Hook config location" decision), it's just as capturable as any other untracked file and just as nonsensical to generate a comprehension question about. Codex found this by running `grasp init` in a repo that didn't already gitignore `.claude/` and observing the new hooks file survive filtering as a 39-line "significant" change. The Phase 9 fix for `.grasp.json` was a point fix, not a generalized "ignore Grasp's own footprint" rule — this closes the other half of that same class of bug the same way, for the same reasons (see the `.grasp.json` entry above for the full "why baseline, not `ignorePatterns`, not gitignore-only" reasoning, which applies identically here).
./DECISIONS.md:577:## 2026-08-05 — Codex fix pass: SQLite busy_timeout — an explicit, generous value, and what it does and doesn't close
./DECISIONS.md:579:**Decision:** `openStore()` now explicitly sets `PRAGMA busy_timeout = 10000` (10 seconds), and a real concurrency regression test (`test/concurrency.test.ts`) spawns 8 separate OS processes writing to the same store and asserts every one succeeds with every row landing — not just that the pragma value reads back correctly.
./DECISIONS.md:581:**Reasoning — the finding that shaped this:** Before writing the fix, `better-sqlite3`'s own source (`node_modules/better-sqlite3/lib/database.js`) was checked directly rather than assumed: its `Database` constructor already defaults an internal `timeout` option to `5000`ms whenever the caller doesn't pass one, which Grasp's `new Database(dbPath)` call never did — meaning a 5-second busy-wait was *already* in effect before this fix, contrary to what this project's own earlier comment here claimed ("without a busy timeout, better-sqlite3's default is to fail immediately," which was itself never verified and turned out to be wrong). Given that, the explicit pragma is not "adding retry behavior where none existed" so much as making an already-present-but-implicit 5s default into an intentional, documented, easily-tunable product decision that survives a future `better-sqlite3` major version silently changing its own default — a real, if less dramatic, reason to keep the change. The chosen value (10s, double the pre-existing default) was settled empirically, not picked round-number-style: a concurrency test spawning 8 real child processes against the *same, already-existing* database (`openStore()` called once, single-process, before the workers start — matching how `grasp init` always establishes the DB before any hook can possibly fire, see below) passed 8/8 times at both 5000ms (the pre-existing default) and 10000ms, so 10s was chosen as a modest safety margin for a slower/loaded machine rather than because 5s was observed to be insufficient for the realistic case.
./DECISIONS.md:583:**A real edge case this does NOT reliably close, and why that's an acceptable, logged tradeoff rather than a bug:** The same concurrency test, run instead against a database file that does **not yet exist** — i.e. several processes racing to call `openStore()` for the very first time simultaneously, each trying to switch a brand-new file into WAL mode via `PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL` — still produced occasional `SQLITE_BUSY` ("database is locked") failures (1-2 out of 8 workers, across several runs) even at busy_timeout values as high as 15000ms. Raising the timeout further did not reliably fix this, which points to a different mechanism than ordinary read/write lock contention: the *first* `journal_mode=WAL` switch on a not-yet-WAL database appears not to fully honor the busy-timeout retry loop the way regular statements do (a documented-elsewhere SQLite subtlety around exclusive locks taken during the journal-mode transition itself, confirmed here empirically rather than by citation). This was not chased further with a code fix because it isn't reachable in Grasp's real architecture: the only place a Grasp database file is ever created for the first time is inside `openStore()`, and the only way for *any* hook-triggered process (`grasp internal:hook`) to run at all is for Claude Code to already have Grasp's hooks registered — which only happens after `grasp init` (a single, one-shot, interactive, never-itself-concurrent command) has already run to completion and already called `openStore()` once, alone. By the time concurrent hook firings become possible, the database is guaranteed to already exist in WAL mode; the "N processes racing to create the file simultaneously" scenario this session found is real, but not something Grasp's own architecture can trigger short of a user manually deleting `~/.grasp/history.db` while other Grasp processes happen to be executing at that exact moment — accepted as an out-of-scope, self-inflicted edge case rather than something worth further engineering. `test/concurrency.test.ts` pre-creates the store before spawning workers specifically to test the *reachable* scenario, with a comment explaining why.
./DECISIONS.md:585:**Alternatives considered:** Leave the pre-existing (undocumented, unverified-by-this-project) `better-sqlite3` default alone and add no explicit pragma at all — rejected once the default was actually confirmed to exist, because leaving it implicit means a future reader (or a future dependency upgrade) has no signal that this value is load-bearing; an explicit, commented pragma is strictly more honest about what's actually relied upon. Chase the WAL-creation-race further (e.g. an even larger timeout, or a file-lock-based "only one process may create the DB" guard) — rejected as solving a problem with no real path to occurring given `grasp init`'s architectural guarantee; adding defensive machinery for a scenario that can only happen via manual file deletion mid-session isn't a good use of complexity budget. A single-process-in-process simulation of concurrency (e.g. two `better-sqlite3` handles in one Node process, one holding an open transaction) instead of real spawned processes — considered, since it would be simpler to write, but rejected per the task's own explicit instruction not to "just assert the pragma is set," and because it wouldn't match Codex's actual repro shape (independent OS processes) as faithfully; real child processes are what actually contend for SQLite's file-level lock the way concurrent `grasp` invocations genuinely would.
./DECISIONS.md:593:**Reasoning:** The prior behavior (`typeof envelope.total_cost_usd === "number" ? envelope.total_cost_usd : 0`) meant a response missing its own cost figure was recorded as a normal, successful, **free** generation — which is a real cap-bypass risk Codex correctly flagged: the cost cap works by summing `events.cost_usd` for a session, and `NULL`/`0` both contribute nothing to that sum, so repeated calls that happen to omit `total_cost_usd` (a malformed/unusual Claude response, not necessarily malicious) could keep generating indefinitely without the cap ever seeing real spend. Discarding the whole response rather than trying to salvage the question while just marking cost unknown was a deliberate choice: brief §3.5's own field list treats `cost_usd` as `NULL` meaning "genuinely unknown," and a response Grasp can't verify the true cost of isn't one it should present to the user as a trustworthy, costed question — this keeps the cap's accounting honest at the cost of occasionally discarding a question that might have been fine, which is the same "never let a suspicious response bypass the cap" posture already established for the `is_error`/malformed-JSON miss paths.
./DECISIONS.md:595:**Alternatives considered:** Keep the question but record `cost_usd: NULL` for that one row — rejected because it would mean cap enforcement quietly stops being accurate the moment any single response is missing its cost, while still showing the user a real question as if nothing were wrong; treating the whole thing as a miss is more conservative and keeps the failure visible (a `miss_reason: "error"` row) rather than silent. Estimate a fallback cost (e.g. the session's average cost-per-call so far) instead of `NULL` — rejected as inventing a number the response never actually reported, which is worse than admitting it's unknown; brief §3.5 already has a vocabulary for "unknown" (`NULL`) and doesn't ask for estimation. Add a fourth `miss_reason` specifically for this case — rejected for the same reason the Phase 8 question-cap entry already gave for not adding a new value: the existing `error` value already means "a response was received but couldn't be trusted/used," which this is, without inventing schema drift the brief didn't ask for.
./DECISIONS.md:601:**Decision:** Added a real automated test suite (51 tests) using Node's built-in test runner (`node:test` + `node:assert/strict`) — no new devDependency. Tests live in `test/*.test.ts` (plus `test/fixtures/`), compiled by a dedicated `tsconfig.test.json` (extends the main config, `rootDir: "."`, `outDir: "test-dist"`) so compiled test output never mixes into the real `dist/` that gets published. `npm test` runs `tsc -p tsconfig.test.json && node --test test-dist/test/*.test.js`. Coverage: the mechanical filter (`test/filter.test.ts` — size thresholds, formatting-only detection, ignore-pattern matching including baseline entries), judge-response parsing and timeout detection (`test/generation.test.ts` — `parseJudgeResponse`, `isTimeoutError`, `buildDiffSummary`), the cost/question cap math end-to-end including the missing-cost fix above (`test/caps.test.ts` — real `openStore` + a controllable mock `claude` fixture on `PATH`, the same technique this project's manual verification has used throughout, now automated), config merge/precedence (`test/config.test.ts` — `deepMerge`, `ensureGlobalConfigFile`, `loadConfig` against temp directories), and the SQLite concurrency fix (`test/concurrency.test.ts`, covered in its own entry above). Three small, narrowly-scoped exports were added purely for testability, all backward-compatible (every existing call site is unaffected): `deepMerge`, `parseJudgeResponse`, and `isTimeoutError` became exported instead of module-private, and `ensureGlobalConfigFile`/`loadConfig` gained optional `globalConfigPath`/`graspHome` parameters (defaulting to the real `~/.grasp` locations) so tests can point them at a temp directory instead of ever touching a real user's config.
./DECISIONS.md:603:**Reasoning:** `node:test` was picked over Jest/Vitest/Mocha for the same reason this project has repeatedly chosen "one fewer dependency" throughout its build (Phase 1's "no CLI argument-parsing framework," the hand-rolled ignore-pattern matcher over `minimatch`, shelling out to `git` directly over `simple-git`): it ships with Node itself (this project already requires Node ≥22, see the `engines.node` fix), needs no transform/config layer beyond the `tsc` compile step the project already has, and is a fully capable, standard test runner for what this suite actually needs (plain assertions, async tests, no snapshot testing or elaborate mocking). A separate `test/`+`tsconfig.test.json` (rather than colocating `*.test.ts` under `src/` and compiling everything together) keeps the *shipped* `dist/` — what `npm pack`/`files: ["dist"]` actually publishes — exactly what it was before this session: no test code, no test fixtures, in the published package. The `caps.test.ts` approach (real `openStore`, real `runGeneration`, a controllable mock `claude` binary on `PATH`) deliberately does not mock or inject anything *inside* `generation.ts` — it proves the actual wiring (cap checks, envelope handling, DB writes) end-to-end the same way this project's manual mock-`claude` verification has throughout, just automated and repeatable instead of hand-run once per phase. The three testability-only exports/parameters were each checked for backward compatibility before being added — none change behavior for any existing caller, all existing call sites already omit the new optional parameters.
./DECISIONS.md:605:**Alternatives considered:** Jest or Vitest — more familiar to many JS developers and richer assertion/mocking ergonomics, but both need either `ts-jest`/Babel or Vitest's own bundler-based transform pipeline, adding real dependency weight and a second build configuration to an already-lean toolchain for capability this suite doesn't need. Colocate tests under `src/` — rejected per the "keep published `dist/` clean" reasoning above. Mock `execFileSync`/the `claude` subprocess call inside `generation.ts` via dependency injection, instead of a real mock binary on `PATH` — considered, since it would remove the need for a fixture script and real process spawns; rejected because it would require restructuring `generation.ts`'s API for the sole benefit of tests, whereas the mock-binary-on-`PATH` approach needed zero changes to production code and is proven, low-risk (it's exactly what this project's own manual verification already relied on since Phase 5).
./DECISIONS.md:613:**Reasoning:** This was a real, previously-accepted gap — `TESTING_GUIDE.md`'s own "Known limitations" section already documented it explicitly ("If you already ran `grasp init` before this build was finished being tested, some internal timing settings won't update automatically... try removing Grasp's hook entries... and running `grasp init` again"), i.e. the fix for a stale install was "manually delete the file and start over," which is exactly the onboarding-friction problem `grasp init`'s own existence is supposed to solve. It's a concrete, reachable scenario in this project's own history, not hypothetical: the Phase 5 timeout bump (15s → 45s, logged separately) means anyone who ran `grasp init` during Phase 3 has been silently sitting on a 15s hook timeout ever since, with no signal anything was ever out of date. Comparing structurally (`entriesEqual`, field-by-field) rather than by re-stringifying and comparing strings matters because an existing entry's JSON key order depends on however it was originally written and isn't guaranteed to match `desiredEntry()`'s own construction order — a string comparison would flag entries as "stale" that are actually semantically identical, which would make every `grasp init` re-run prompt for a no-op "update."
./DECISIONS.md:615:**Alternatives considered:** Always silently overwrite any existing Grasp entry with the current desired shape on every `grasp init` run, no diff shown — rejected per the same "trust is load-bearing, show what's changing" reasoning that already governs the missing-entry case (the original "Hook registration mechanics" decision); a user should see a timeout value change just as much as they'd see a wholly new hook being added. Version-stamp the installed config (e.g. embed a schema/version field in the hook entry itself) and compare versions instead of structural equality — rejected as needless indirection: the entries are small and fully known ahead of time (`DESIRED`), so a direct structural comparison is simpler and can't drift out of sync with a separately-maintained version number. Detect staleness but only warn, requiring the user to manually delete and re-run — rejected as not actually fixing the underlying friction problem this fix exists to close; an automatic, confirmed in-place update is what "one command" installation is supposed to mean.
./prompts/test_prompt_template.txt:6:2. Test it thoroughly and skeptically -- don't just check that commands exit without crashing, form a real judgment about whether each piece actually does what it claims to do. Cover at minimum: diff capture and mechanical filtering, question generation (using a mock `claude` binary if no real authenticated CLI is available -- check the codebase for the established mocking pattern from earlier test rounds), cost cap and question-count cap enforcement (including that they accumulate correctly across a whole session, not per turn), `grasp review`'s answer/skip flow (including that blank answers are rejected, not silently accepted), gate modes (soft vs. hard), failure/timeout handling, config loading and per-repo overrides, and a fresh-eyes read of the code and docs for any remaining inconsistencies, bugs, or misleading claims.
./prompts/test_prompt_template.txt:7:3. Do not modify or read the developer's real ~/.grasp/history.db -- use a scratch $HOME/scratch repos exactly as established in prior test rounds, and restore/clean up everything when you're done.
