# Codex independent test report

## Summary

Grasp builds, starts, and works correctly in a normal sequential happy path. It captured incremental edits without repeating an unchanged diff, filtered several common kinds of noise, generated and stored mock questions, remembered an answered concept across repositories, enforced both configured limits across sequential turns in one Claude session, presented a readable review screen, rejected blank answers, saved real answers and deliberate skips, and made the basic soft/hard gate decisions correctly.

This run still found release-blocking defects. Most seriously, simultaneous `PostToolUse` hooks can all claim the same edit and all make a paid Claude call. Eight overlapping hooks created eight copies of one question, spent $0.016 despite a $0.003 cost limit, and exceeded a two-question limit with no cap miss. Repository overrides also disappear when Claude runs from a subdirectory, so hard mode and both safety limits can be bypassed. A valid JSON config with the wrong field type crashed the hook and permanently lost the edit it was processing.

Other real failures remain: edited renames in nested paths are counted as zero lines and discarded; obvious generated files and common formatter line wrapping reach paid generation; a brand-new concept can be presented without the required concept-first question; and Git cleanup can delete Grasp's stored checkpoint and leave that session unable to capture later work.

I would not rely on this build for real work yet.


